---
import { LINKS } from '../config';

/** Every inline link here, matching the step links in Install. */
const LINK_CLASS =
	'font-bold text-stone-700 underline decoration-stone-300 underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:text-stone-900 hover:decoration-stone-500';

const ANSWER_CLASS = 'text-[15px] leading-relaxed font-medium text-stone-600';

/**
 * The same answers as plain text, for search engines. Kept next to the markup
 * so a changed answer is changed in both places in one edit.
 */
const faqLd = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	mainEntity: [
		[
			'What does it cost?',
			"Nothing, for a personal instance. There is no subscription and no account with us: you deploy it to your own Cloudflare account, on Cloudflare's free plan. Cloudflare asks for a card on file for R2, the bucket that stores your images; Workers and D1 never ask for one, and you are only charged if you go past the free limits."
		],
		[
			'What happens if I hit a Cloudflare limit?',
			'R2 keeps the first 10 GB free and traffic out is always free. The free plan allows five cron triggers per account, shared with every other Worker; if they are taken, drive the scheduler from your own timer. D1 pauses for the day past 100,000 rows written, resumes at midnight UTC, and nothing is lost.'
		],
		[
			'Do I need a developer app for every platform?',
			'No. Mastodon and Bluesky connect with what you already have. LinkedIn, Threads and X need an app you register with them first: the app walks you through it, takes the credentials in a form, and can hand the setup to a browser agent. X also charges for posting: its API is pay-per-use, so fund a small balance before you connect it. If you would rather not register apps, you can connect them through Zernio instead. It has a free plan, though X needs a card there.'
		],
		[
			'Can AI agents like Claude Code use CogSend?',
			'Yes. Your instance includes an MCP server, so Claude Code, Codex or any MCP client can draft, schedule and publish with your personal API key. A read-only key lets an agent look without posting.'
		],
		[
			'How do I update it later?',
			'CogSend tells you when a release is out, with a dot on your avatar and a line in Settings. Installed with the Deploy to Cloudflare button, run the Update CogSend action in your repository, or let it install patch releases by itself; no Cloudflare token is needed. Any install can also update from Settings with a Cloudflare token, which it can remember, locked with your password, or from a checkout with npm run deploy:release. Your posts and secrets live in your Cloudflare account, so an update cannot touch them.'
		],
		[
			'Where do my posts and credentials live?',
			'In your Cloudflare account, in the database and bucket the deploy created. Platform tokens are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM, and the single admin account signs in with an authenticator app and backup codes.'
		]
	].map(([name, text]) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name,
		acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text }
	}))
};
---

<!-- Questions people ask before running this on their own account. The cost
     answer used to be a card in Install; it belongs here, as an answer to the
     question a reader is actually asking. -->
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />
<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[1180px] px-6 py-20 sm:py-32" id="faq">
	<div class="mx-auto mb-14 max-w-2xl text-center">
		<p class="mb-4 text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest text-stone-500 uppercase">Questions</p>
		<h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-4xl">
			Before you deploy.
		</h2>
	</div>

	<ul class="mx-auto max-w-2xl" data-reveal>
		<li class="border-t border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				What does it cost?
			</h3>
			<p class={ANSWER_CLASS}>
				Nothing, for a personal instance. There is no subscription, nothing to cancel, and no account with us — you
				deploy it to your own Cloudflare account, on Cloudflare's free plan. A personal instance
				stays well inside it.
			</p>
			<p class={`mt-3 ${ANSWER_CLASS}`}>
				The one thing Cloudflare asks for is a card on file for R2, the bucket that stores your
				images. Workers and D1 never ask for one, and you are only charged if you go past the free
				limits.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li class="border-t border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				What happens if I hit a Cloudflare limit?
			</h3>
			<ul class="space-y-3 text-[14px] leading-relaxed font-medium text-stone-600">
				<li>
					<span class="font-bold text-stone-800">Storage.</span> R2 keeps the first 10 GB free, and
					traffic out is always free.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-bold text-stone-800">Cron triggers.</span> Five per account, shared with
					every other Worker you run. If they are taken, drive the scheduler from your own timer
					instead.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-bold text-stone-800">The database.</span> D1 pauses for the day past
					100,000 rows written. It resumes at midnight UTC, and nothing is lost.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed font-medium text-stone-600">
				Numbers can change:{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.cfWorkersLimits} class={LINK_CLASS}>Workers limits</a>,{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.cfD1Pricing} class={LINK_CLASS}>D1</a>,{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.cfR2Pricing} class={LINK_CLASS}>R2</a>.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li class="border-t border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				Do I need a developer app for every platform?
			</h3>
			<p class={ANSWER_CLASS}>
				No. Mastodon and Bluesky connect with what you already have. LinkedIn, Threads and X need an
				app you register with them first, because they issue the client id and secret your Worker
				uses. Pick one and the app shows you exactly what to create, takes the credentials in a
				form, and can hand the whole setup to a browser agent.
			</p>
			<p class={`mt-3 ${ANSWER_CLASS}`}>
				X also charges for posting. Its API is pay-per-use, so fund a small balance before you
				connect it. See{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.oauth} class={LINK_CLASS}>
					OAuth app setup
				</a>
				.
			</p>
			<p class={`mt-3 ${ANSWER_CLASS}`}>
				If you would rather not register apps, you can connect them{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.zernioDocs} class={LINK_CLASS}>through Zernio</a> instead. It has a free
				plan, though X needs a card there.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li class="border-t border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				Can AI agents like Claude Code use CogSend?
			</h3>
			<p class={ANSWER_CLASS}>
				Yes. Your instance includes an MCP server, so Claude Code, Codex or any MCP client can draft,
				schedule and publish with your personal API key. A read-only key lets an agent look without
				posting. See{' '}
				<a href={`${LINKS.api}#mcp-server`} class={LINK_CLASS}>MCP server</a>.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li class="border-t border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				How do I update it later?
			</h3>
			<p class={ANSWER_CLASS}>
				CogSend tells you when a release is out, with a dot on your avatar and a line in Settings.
				Installed with the Deploy to Cloudflare button, run the <strong>Update CogSend</strong> action
				in your repository, which checks the release's signature and lets Cloudflare deploy it, or
				let it install patch releases by itself. No Cloudflare token is needed.
			</p>
			<p class={`mt-3 ${ANSWER_CLASS}`}>
				Any install can also update from Settings with a Cloudflare token, which it can remember,
				locked with your password, or from a checkout with <code>npm run deploy:release</code>. Your posts, images and secrets live in your Cloudflare account, so an update cannot
				touch them. See{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.updates} class={LINK_CLASS}>Updating</a>.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li class="border-t border-b border-stone-200/80 py-7">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-[17px] font-extrabold tracking-tight text-stone-900">
				Where do my posts and credentials live?
			</h3>
			<p class={ANSWER_CLASS}>
				In your Cloudflare account, in the database and bucket the deploy created for you. Platform
				tokens are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM, and the single admin account signs in with an
				authenticator app and backup codes. Nothing about your instance lives with us.
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
</section>
